<article class="ax-lab ax-methods">
<span class="ax-kicker">BIOHYBRID AXON RESOLUTION / STAGE 2</span><h1>MODALITIES & SELECTIVITY: METHODS</h1>
<p><a href="../">Open coupled laboratory</a> · <a href="../questions/">Scientific questions</a> · <a href="../validation.json">Full numerical report</a></p>
<div class="ax-boundary"><strong>{report.completeStage2?'Numerical Stage 2 release gates passed.':'Development checkpoint: Stage 2 release gates remain open.'}</strong><p>Active myelinated cables, physical wells, magnetic induction and photon-driven membrane currents are calculated. Engineered ganglion physiology, survival, growth, chronic response and manufacturing are uncalibrated. Each introduced bipolar cell has its own imposed projections; this B3 experiment contains no organoid-to-host junction.</p></div>
<h2>01 · Scope and identity</h2>
<p>Every declared cell, channel and sampled amplitude is solved. The worker runs matched zero-input, regional active-conductance removal, soma-only removal and whole-cell removal controls. Optical runs add an opsin block that keeps the light field and all native conductances. Whole-cell native silencing keeps the photocurrent. All interventions begin from the same saved native state. A change in controls cannot relabel an earlier result.</p>
<p>Original host identities H and introduced identities E remain distinct. Complete ganglion resection makes original-host continuity and Y<sub>axon</sub> zero. An electrically recruited E output is not restored H continuity. The matched no-device condition has the same resection and no introduced cells, well walls or drive. Growth and target reinnervation are not inferred.</p>
<h2>02 · Three physical drives</h2>
<p><b>Electrode · mathematical.</b> The Stage 1 conforming tetrahedral FEM resolves finite source/return contacts, insulating wells, conducting apertures, anisotropic endoneurium and perineurium. Charge-balanced biphasic drive uses the same physical transfer and double-cable model. Geometric phase charge is reported; electrode polarization and electrochemical safety are unsolved.</p>
<pre>{'∇ · (σ ∇φ) = 0 with explicit current boundary loads\nφe(s,t) = T(s) I(t)\nCk = cable potential range at target / maximum off-target range'}</pre>
<p><b>Microcoil · mathematical formulation, hypothetical geometry.</b> Analytic finite segments include every winding and return segment. The complete finite-width winding lies inside the pre-existing excluded wall; a wire is never silently placed in conducting tissue. The primary electric field drives an independently assembled conservative FEM load. Charge redistribution at the tissue and insulation boundaries adds a secondary scalar field.</p>
<pre>{'A(r) = μ0 I/(4π) ∫wire dl′ / |r − r′|\nEp = −∂A/∂t\nKij = ∫ grad(Ni) · σ grad(Nj) dV\nbi = ∫ grad(Ni) · σ Ep dV\nK φ = b\nψ(s) = φ(s) + (dI/dt) ∫path A1 · dl\nEparallel = −dψ/ds; activation depends on its spatial variation.'}</pre>
<p>The Stage 2 induction solver applies symmetric diagonal equilibration to the same physical stiffness matrix. IC(0) shifts affect only its preconditioner. PCG checks the unscaled true residual without overwriting its recurrence; any residual replacement restarts the search direction. The true-residual limit remains 10⁻¹⁰, the recurrence target 2 × 10⁻¹² and the iteration cap 16,000. Stage 1 source is unchanged.</p>
<p>A nonmagnetic wall does not shield the primary magnetic field. It still affects secondary electric fields through the conductivity boundary. The path potential is valid for this straight chain. Transverse soma polarization, branched-cycle fields, skin effects and driver parasitics are unresolved. The coupled axis-aligned sidewall coil can couple very weakly; a negative result for it is not a negative result for all magnetic geometries. Wire resistance, approximate inductance, pulse energy and aggregate drive power remain visible.</p>
<p><b>Optical · mathematical transport, hypothetical tissue.</b> Seeded Lambertian packets scatter with the Henyey–Greenstein law, absorb in a uniform medium and reflect from an opaque specular liner. Geometric apertures determine escape. Weighted path length in each thin spherical soma band gives local scalar fluence. The physical cell and field anatomy are inherited unchanged.</p>
<pre>{'Φband / Pemitted = Σhistories(weight × path length in band) / (N × shell volume)\nIopsin = gmax × area × [10.6408 − 14.6408 exp(−Vm / 42.7671)] × (O1 + 0.1 O2)\nC1 ⇄ O1 ⇄ O2 ⇄ C2 → C1\nProbability-conserving Markov update; voltage-dependent rate and current equations.\nThe photocurrent enters only the axolemma balance, never the outer/periaxonal balance.'}</pre>
<p>The H134R rates follow Williams et al. and the ModelDB implementation. Those data do not fit engineered human ganglion cells. Expression density is a hypothetical control. Transport supplies local exposure, so the additional loss divisor is one. Native conductances and opsin states interact during every time step; responses are not assigned from a light threshold.</p>
<h2>03 · Optical uncertainty and spectral boundary</h2>
<p>Each photon is an independent bounded contribution. The maximum shell chord and maximum packet-retention ratio bound its total contribution. A two-sided empirical Bernstein interval allocates a family error probability across all requested channels and bands. Zero detected off-target photons produce a positive upper bound. Discarded packet weight remains in the energy budget and worst-case confidence tail.</p>
<p>Lower, nominal and upper exposure vectors are passed through the nonlinear cable. If recruitment differs, the headline is withheld. Agreement of three probes does not enclose every interior nonlinear response and is never described as a rigorous firing guarantee. Shell thickness, finite-domain loss, optical coefficients and biological uncertainty are separate from packet-sampling uncertainty.</p>
<p>The component assays include spectral interpolation and two-to-four hypothetical action spectra with an explicit blue tail. These are engineering sensitivity examples. Calibrated multi-opsin kinetics and wavelength-dependent full-cohort recruitment are not yet integrated; no spectral channel multiplier is counted.</p>
<h2>04 · Causal selectivity and recording</h2>
<p>The primary figure is the full channel-by-cell binary recruitment matrix at each declared amplitude. Three ordered distal-node crossings with plausible outward delays define qualifying propagation. This is temporal evidence, not proof of initiation location. A channel is exclusive only if its complete assigned row contains one output. Required mechanism-removal controls must abolish that output. An off-target cannot be filtered out to manufacture exclusivity.</p>
<p>Four metrics are retained: conditional exclusive outputs, shared-only outputs, exact matrix rank over the real numbers, and deterministic mutual information for equiprobable off/isolated-channel trials. Rank is not nonlinear controllability; information is not bits per second or a noise-robust capacity. Adjacent valid amplitude samples bracket observed transitions without interpolation, monotonicity assumptions or bridging failed points. Magnetic headlines, operating points and qualified threshold brackets remain withheld because the induction field fails mesh refinement; nominal patterns and controls are retained as diagnostics.</p>
<p>Full-precision transmembrane source currents are projected through the reciprocal recording-contact functional. Engineered and original-host contributions remain separate. Electrode artifact and neural signal are exported individually and summed. Optical imposed electrical artifact is zero within this model, while LED wiring pickup is unmodeled. Magnetic artifact and total recording are withheld because the measurement-lead loop is unspecified.</p>
<h2>05 · Numerical evidence and fixed acceptance</h2>
